{
  "abstract": "At each state of a shortcut Collatz orbit, we compare its elapsed clock with the fastest arrival from any smaller positive start. Sample uniformly from starts in $Y<x<3Y/2$ whose orbits survive without strict descent through clock $m$, with $Y\\ge 2^m$. A finite parity lookup computes the positive savings and their exact arrival clocks. Using interval selection and the first aligned agreement of the two paths, we bound late improvements and prove that the saving at clock $i$ converges in total variation to a single law $\\pi$ when both $i$ and $m-i$ diverge, including $m-i=o(m)$. The mass at zero is positive. The positive support consists exactly of the integers $h=\\lceil n\\log_2 3\\rceil$ for which the next ceiling jumps by two, with $n\\ge1$. Each such mass has order $(1+h)^{-3/2}$, and we determine the earliest clock of each attainable saving. The finite-clock tail through $L\\le i/5$ has order $(L+1)^{-1/2}+(m-i+1)^{-1/2}$, and the mean has order $\\sqrt{i}+i/\\sqrt{m-i+1}$. At the terminal clock $m$, a positive fraction admit a visitor in at most 173 steps. Every subsequential law of the saving divided by $m$ places positive masses at zero and one. We give the mixture weights along generic phases through the endpoint height, a reversed conditioned walk and a ternary residue. The formula leaves resonant phases and variation of the weight across phases open. Twelve exact numerical audits reproduce the finite checks.",
